{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 410,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to CuriosityStream for supporting PBS\nDigital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4330,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bad ideas come and go in physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6740,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s one bit of nonsense that is\nperhaps more persistent than all others: the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11620,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perpetual motion machine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19100,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No working perpetual motion machine has ever\nbeen experiment verified."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23740,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All break the laws of thermodynamics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26300,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, we classify them based on WHICH law of\nthermodynamics they break."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30970,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have perpetual motion machines of the first\nkind - they violate energy conservation - they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36670,
    "dDurationMs": 3299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pump more energy out than they need to keep\nrunning."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39969,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This includes most of the historical devices."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43579,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then there are machines of the second kind\n- they’re a bit more subtle in their wrongness"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48699,
    "dDurationMs": 6411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because break the second law of thermodynamics\n- extracting energy by reversing entropy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55110,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many modern “free-energy” devices fall\ninto this category."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59570,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the best modern designs are by you - answers\nto our recent challenge question, which we’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67190,
    "dDurationMs": 1220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get to at the end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68410,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But first let’s take a look at examples\nof what other people came up with - this’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be a fun little journey through some pretty\nterrible science."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78480,
    "dDurationMs": 2890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Designing a perpetual motion machine has a\nvery long history and became quite the craze"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81370,
    "dDurationMs": 2410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the middle ages through the renaissance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83780,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first well-documented design for a perpetual\nmotion machine was from the 12th century."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87530,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bhāskara's wheel, named after the Indian\nmathematician, was embedded with tubes of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92380,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mercury that would flow from back and forth\nas the wheel turned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96070,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Other types of over-balance wheels followed\nthrough to the Renaissance and worked on the same principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100640,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Basically the shift in the center of gravity of the spokes on one side should drive the wheel around."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107060,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There were also designs that employed the\nmagical-seeming lodestones – magnets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112440,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, this ramp in which a ball is\npulled to the top by a magnet before falling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117670,
    "dDurationMs": 2670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through a hole and rolling to the bottom again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120340,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then there are the self-pumping waterwheels\nor self-blowing windmills."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125110,
    "dDurationMs": 2790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "None of these machines actually worked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127900,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In every case you can find a subtle bit of\nphysics that the designers overlooked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132880,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The overbalance wheel pushes masses outwards\non one side, but that same at the same time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137530,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increases the separation between those masses,\nso that moment of inertia is unchanged."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142360,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A magnet strong enough to pull a ball up anincline would also prevent it from falling through that hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149040,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Losses due to friction mean that water or\nwind driving a mill could never produce enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154360,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy to fully complete the cycle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157220,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Close, but no perpetual lighting cigar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161100,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now to be fair to the people working during\nthe middle ages and Renaissance, the law of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165380,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conservation of energy hadn’t been discovered\nyet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168130,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thus the proliferation of first-type machines\nthat could generate more energy than they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172780,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "used."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173780,
    "dDurationMs": 8870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But absolute proof of their non-viability, by the discovery of the first order of thermodynamics in the 18th century,\ndidn’t end the craze."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182650,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Inventors just graduated to instead breaking the second\nlaw of thermodynamics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187790,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To review: the 2nd law states that entropy\ncan never decrease over time – in other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193180,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "words, energy will tend to spread itself out\nas evenly as possible – to a state of maximum entropy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All machines work by riding this flow of energy\nas it redistributes itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206810,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Machines of the second kind claim to be able\nto tap reservoirs of energy that are already"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212310,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evenly spread – effectively reversing the\ngrowth of entropy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217130,
    "dDurationMs": 6490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get to modern perpetual motion machines,\nlet’s take a look at some actually interesting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223620,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ideas that illustrate the limit of the second\nlaw."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227760,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up, the Brownian Ratchet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229910,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It goes like this: a paddle wheel immersed\nin a gas is connected to a cog with a latch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236790,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that only lets it turn in one direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240360,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Individual gas particles are moving around\nwith random – or Brownian motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245630,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When one hits the wheel in one direction the\ncog turns, but in the other direction the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250360,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotation is blocked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252060,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sounds … plausible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254320,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it actually works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256460,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But ONLY if the chamber containing the cog\nis at a lower temperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260980,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it’s at the same temperature then the\ncog is as likely to be pushed back when the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265320,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "latch raises."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266820,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Richard Feynman himself demonstrated this\nmathematically."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270770,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is actually a specific example of a Maxwell’s\ndemon device, an entropy-reversing thought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276750,
    "dDurationMs": 2669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiment that we’ve discussed before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279419,
    "dDurationMs": 4211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With no energy or temperature gradient, no\nenergy can be extracted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283630,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact there’s an absolute limit to the\namount of energy that can be extracted based"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288380,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a difference in temperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290900,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That limit is defined by the most important\n– perhaps the only important perpetual motion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296480,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system ever conceived: the Carnot cycle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301030,
    "dDurationMs": 6919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the early 19th century, Sadi Carnot described\nthe most efficient possible engine – It’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307949,
    "dDurationMs": 5081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a sequence of expansion and contraction of\ngas in a piston chamber that will provide"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313030,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the maximum possible energy as heat flows\nbetween reservoirs of different temperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318980,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Critically, the Carnot cycle is reversible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Drive the piston in reverse with exactly the\nsame amount of energy, and it’ll transfer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327960,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy from cold to hot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330480,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In principle a Carnot engine could extract\nenergy from a temperature gradient and then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335500,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pump it back in again, making it a candidate\nperpetual motion machine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340650,
    "dDurationMs": 8470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even in this most ideal case the ratio\nof energy in to energy out is exactly unity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349120,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No extra energy is extracted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352600,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Carnot cycle is, in principle, a perpetual\nmotion process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But doesn’t break any laws of thermodynamics,\nso it’s not the first or second kind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363120,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact it’s the third kind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366000,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are perpetual motion machines that do\nnot attempt to generate energy – they just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371020,
    "dDurationMs": 2149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keep themselves running forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373169,
    "dDurationMs": 1031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sounds OK, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374200,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, Newton tells us, an object in motion\ntends to remain in motion unless acted on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379460,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by an outside force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381240,
    "dDurationMs": 5691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So remove all outside forces – remove all\nfriction, all energy losses of any kind, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386931,
    "dDurationMs": 3809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surely any machine can tick on forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390740,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Indeed there are ideal systems that, from\npure classical principles should run forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396760,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like a perfect Carnot engine, or a frictionless\nwheel – with or without magnets and mercury"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402250,
    "dDurationMs": 2690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tubes – or a planet orbiting a star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404940,
    "dDurationMs": 1900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just eliminate ANY leakage of energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406840,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s not just an engineering challenge,\nit’s fundamentally impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410910,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, quantum mechanics forbids it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415750,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Due to the intrinsic quantum randomness of\nall particles, as expressed by the Heisenberg"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420000,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty principle, everything moves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423800,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Internal parts always vibrate, guaranteeing\nsome friction and internal heat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428470,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if that heat is recaptured, the outer\nwalls of even the most carefully isolated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433370,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system must radiate, slowly leaking energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437330,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then of course there’s the gravitational\nradiation that will slowly sap the “perpetual"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442320,
    "dDurationMs": 3050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "motion” of celestial systems."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445370,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The best-case scenario for a perpetual motion\nmachine of the third kind is a very, very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450980,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slow wind-down to stillness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454120,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that we've reviewed three types of possible wrongness in perpetual motion machines,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459200,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let's look at some of the modern proposals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462520,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most look a lot like their ancient predecessors\n– often lots of wheels with lots of magnets,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468110,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "somehow powering their own motion and producing\nextra energy besides."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472930,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are often referred to as over-unity\ndevices – the ratio of energy out to energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478470,
    "dDurationMs": 2350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in is greater than one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480820,
    "dDurationMs": 3719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That name was actually coined by the US patent\noffice, which along with the UK, patent office"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484539,
    "dDurationMs": 4571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the French Academy of Science refuses\nto even review devices claiming over-unity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489110,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy production."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490980,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Understandably - it eliminates 50% of their\ncrank submissions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495800,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the purveyors of over-unity devices typically\ndo NOT claim to have created perpetual motion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501780,
    "dDurationMs": 2050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "devices of the first kind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503830,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They have various explanations for how they\ndon’t violate conservation of energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509050,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However most end up shifting their device\nto the second kind of perpetual motion: they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514270,
    "dDurationMs": 6809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "violate the 2nd law of thermodynamics by extracting\nenergy where there is no energy gradient."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521079,
    "dDurationMs": 5891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One popular source of free energy is the zero-point\nenergy of the quantum vacuum, and it’s the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526970,
    "dDurationMs": 1309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most fun to debunk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528279,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m going to have to refer you to our entire\nplaylist on the quantum vacuum for the physics,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533459,
    "dDurationMs": 6951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the important point is that any energy\nof the vacuum is exactly the same everywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540410,
    "dDurationMs": 4849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It has no energy gradient, so isn’t accessible\nas an energy source."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545259,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now it may be possible to create an energy\ngradient, for example with the Casimir effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550009,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which plates are pulled together by lowering\nthe zero-point energy between them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555059,
    "dDurationMs": 5691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But doing so repeatedly in an engine cycle\ntakes at least as much energy as it produces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560750,
    "dDurationMs": 5929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At best you have a perpetual motion device\nof the third kind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566679,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most modern perpetual motion builders aren’t\nso technical."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570629,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact the worst of them wouldn’t have\nphysics or engineering chops to build a decent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575529,
    "dDurationMs": 1790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over-balance wheel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577319,
    "dDurationMs": 7390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They rely more on pseudo-physics-speak - zero-point,\nmag-grav, plasma, torus vibrations sound fancy,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584709,
    "dDurationMs": 5531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but are typically less good at generating\nfree energy than they are at directing you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590240,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to their donate button or selling you a telluric\nfield wellness crystal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594410,
    "dDurationMs": 5399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, enough with the nonsense . Let’s get\nto the challenge question, in which I asked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599809,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "YOU to come up with a perpetual motion machine\nbased on the funky notion of negative mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606129,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to some interpretations, negative\nmass should be attracted to positive mass,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611709,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but positive mass should be repelled by negative\nmass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615389,
    "dDurationMs": 5961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The result is that a positive and negative\nmass should accelerate indefinitely, potentially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621350,
    "dDurationMs": 2609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "powering an infinite energy device."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623959,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how to build one?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626209,
    "dDurationMs": 2341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see what you came up with."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628550,
    "dDurationMs": 7310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of you hit on the most obvious and efficient\napproach: attaching the apples to a dynamo"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635860,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so they chase each other in circles, driving\nthe generator."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639610,
    "dDurationMs": 2659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here are some of the better designs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642269,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nice adaption of the Bhāskara's wheel from\nCreepy Magician."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645689,
    "dDurationMs": 5411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alex Taylor has an especially cool dynamo\ndevice in which the masses are contained in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651100,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652550,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He also does the full relativistic calculation\nfor power – nice one Alex."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658120,
    "dDurationMs": 6430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have a rare non-dynamo device from Adrien\nRomeo, who uses cogs with negative and positive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664550,
    "dDurationMs": 1519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass teeth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666069,
    "dDurationMs": 5901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This post-it will be framed in the Smithsonian\nafter the Romeo-engine solves humanity’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671970,
    "dDurationMs": 1679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy crisis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673649,
    "dDurationMs": 5961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the prize for most outlandish goes to\nEpsilon Centauri, whose machine requires an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679610,
    "dDurationMs": 7060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire bubble universe with closed, pac-man\nborders, trapping the ever-accelerating apples."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686670,
    "dDurationMs": 3229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you saw your name and device just now,\nthat means you’re a winner!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689899,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You do get your pick from the Space Time store."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692519,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Shoot us an email at pbspacetime@gmail.com\nand we’ll get your goodies to you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697339,
    "dDurationMs": 4921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Feel free to check the store even if you didn’t\nwin, or think you should’ve won, or didn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702260,
    "dDurationMs": 3169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get around to submitting but are pretty sure\nyou would’ve won if you did."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705429,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We all deserve merch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707499,
    "dDurationMs": 1090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708589,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m also going to post some of the best\nsolutions to this challenge on Instagram – check"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713559,
    "dDurationMs": 2361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "‘em out at matt_of_earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715920,
    "dDurationMs": 4089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And I leave you with wise words from someone\nwho designed quite a few perpetual motion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720009,
    "dDurationMs": 1570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "machines himself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721579,
    "dDurationMs": 1231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Leonardo da Vinci."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722810,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“Oh ye seekers after perpetual motion, how\nmany vain chimeras have you pursued?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727700,
    "dDurationMs": 3639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Go and take your place with the alchemists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731339,
    "dDurationMs": 5831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“ da Vinci was ahead of his time in some\nways – he knew perpetual motion was bunk"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737170,
    "dDurationMs": 3139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even before the laws of thermodynamics came\nalong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740309,
    "dDurationMs": 1411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But vain chimeras?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741720,
    "dDurationMs": 4909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Had only da Vinci known that his own perpetual\nmotion machines could’ve score him such"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746629,
    "dDurationMs": 2591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cool swag from Space Time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749220,
    "dDurationMs": 4789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to CuriosityStream for supporting\nPBS Digital Studios."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754009,
    "dDurationMs": 3971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CuriosityStream is a subscription streaming\nservice that offers documentaries and nonfiction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757980,
    "dDurationMs": 5279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "titles from a variety of filmmakers, including\nCuriosityStream originals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763259,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example you can check out the Destination\nPluto, which follow the timeline of the New"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768399,
    "dDurationMs": 5101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Horizons mission from its inception to the\ndate of its close encounter with everyone's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773500,
    "dDurationMs": 2819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "favorite Kuiper Belt dwarf planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776319,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can learn more at curiositystream dot\ncom slash spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781079,
    "dDurationMs": 4221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey guys, quick announcement - if you’re\nin New York this week I’m going a fun science"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785300,
    "dDurationMs": 3969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing this Thursday, 9pm at Caveat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789269,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s called astronaut training, and it should\nbe hilarious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793459,
    "dDurationMs": 2231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, my cohosts should be hilarious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795690,
    "dDurationMs": 2639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m going to try to not look silly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798329,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m not hopeful on that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799999,
    "dDurationMs": 1981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801980,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, and on to comments from our last episode\n- the Secrets of the Cosmic Microwave Background!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807680,
    "dDurationMs": 5719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In which we deciphered the cryptic patterns\nembedded in the oldest light in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813399,
    "dDurationMs": 5081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Francois Lacombe asks how astronomers distinguish\nthe very small temperature differences in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818480,
    "dDurationMs": 4299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the CMB from the rest of the microwave noise."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822779,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a great question because isolating\nthe CMB is a huge challenge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827149,
    "dDurationMs": 6271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The contaminating microwaves mostly come from\nour galaxy - there's a lot from the dust in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833420,
    "dDurationMs": 4789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between the stars, and also from individual\nelectrons either bumping into other charged"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838209,
    "dDurationMs": 6670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles or circling in magnetic fields Fortunately\nwe can model that stuff pretty well."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844879,
    "dDurationMs": 4301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know where the sources of this emission\nare from observations at other frequencies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849180,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and can do pretty well modeling and subtracting\nthem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853490,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All that galactic emission doesn't look anything\nlike the CMB in the way it fluctuations, so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859500,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's pretty clear when we've subtracted it\nproperly and only the CMB is left."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865170,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Abe Dillon asks whether the \"moment\" of recombination\nshould depend on the local density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870290,
    "dDurationMs": 3979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is absolutely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874269,
    "dDurationMs": 4711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "People tend to talk about recombination as\nhaving happened instantaneously and at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878980,
    "dDurationMs": 2159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same time everywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881139,
    "dDurationMs": 2010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's not the case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883149,
    "dDurationMs": 5271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Recombination happened when the universe became\ncool enough nuclei capture electrons to form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888420,
    "dDurationMs": 1639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first atoms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890059,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More dense regions were a little hotter, so\nrecombination started later in those."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894699,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in general the entire universe didn't\nmake this transition instantaneously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899879,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It took several tens of thousand years - which\nis a lot considering the universe was less"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904259,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than 400,000 years old."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907129,
    "dDurationMs": 5781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It took long enough that the patterns of the\nbaryon acoustic oscillations frozen into both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912910,
    "dDurationMs": 6989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the CMB and in galaxy rings were blurred out\nquite a bit because the universe took a moment"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919899,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to become fully transparent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922259,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Julio Toboso García points out that the method\nused to model the CMB fluctuations - multipole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928029,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion - sounds a like Fourier Analysis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932009,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Good insight, Julio."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933439,
    "dDurationMs": 5741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact it's pretty much exactly that, but\nfor the surface of a sphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939180,
    "dDurationMs": 5349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fourier analysis represents functions in one\ndimension with a series of sine curves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944529,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On a spherical surface you instead use spherical\nharmonics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948769,
    "dDurationMs": 6301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "John Rodriguez wished he'd've known that the\nPhysics skill tree is required to unlock the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955070,
    "dDurationMs": 1259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wizard class."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956329,
    "dDurationMs": 6630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if wizarding schools existed - and they\ndon't, okay, that's just silly - then they're"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962959,
    "dDurationMs": 3901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably more like Brakebills in The Magicians\nby Lev Grossman."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966860,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Less like Hogwarts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968920,
    "dDurationMs": 4439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So basically like the most obnoxiously elite\nivy league grad program."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973359,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You need 5 letters of reference from Nobel\nLaureates, be in the top 0% on your GREs,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978939,
    "dDurationMs": 5491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and yeah, first authorship on a paper in theoretical\nphysics from that high-school science fair"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984430,
    "dDurationMs": 5229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "project."
    } ]
  } ]
}
